Judge a B-spline face's mesh by where it is, not by where its parameters are.

What it fixes
  Two places in BRepMesh assume a surface's parameterization is uniform,
  and a face swept from a curve that starts at rest — an involute on its
  base circle, whose speed in the roll angle is zero there, drawn exactly
  as a cubic B-spline and extruded as a ruled wall — breaks both.

  1. BRepMesh_DelaunayDeflectionControlMeshAlgo checks a triangle at the
     surface point of its parametric centroid and a link at the surface
     point of its parametric middle, against the triangle's plane and the
     link's line. On such a wall the parametric middle of a link running up
     it lies along the wall from the link's own middle by rb·dt²/8 (0.014 mm
     on a module 2 gear's knot span), which reads as deflection the link
     does not have. Every pass splits those links, the new diagonals fail
     the same way, and a flank that meshes in two rows of triangles took
     450. The parametric middle can also miss where an element does stray,
     and a face is left outside the deflection.

     The sample is now moved to the foot of the perpendicular from the
     element's own middle (link midpoint, triangle centroid), by
     Gauss-Newton from the parametric middle, kept only if it converges
     inside the element's parametric box; otherwise the old point is used.
     A sample within the deflection of the element's middle passes either
     way — its foot is nearer still — so the iteration runs only for points
     that would fail. A node inserted for a failing element goes to that
     foot.

  2. BRepMesh_NURBSRangeSplitter adds a row of surface nodes between every
     pair of knots when the normal cannot be computed directly at a knot.
     It asked that of the knots on the face's range boundary too, where no
     surface node is ever inserted (only nodes classified inside the face
     are); the zero-speed edge of such a wall is exactly that, and the added
     row at v = 0.5 doubled every flank's triangles. Only knots strictly
     inside the range are asked now.

How it was measured
  parcad's eval corpus and seed parts, 111 solids read back from STEP,
  meshed at 0.01 mm linear deflection, every triangle sampled at 15
  barycentric points projected onto its face's surface (a local
  projection, so an upper bound on the distance). Before -> after:
  - no part's worst sample moved further from its surface; 20 moved
    closer, 7 of them from outside 0.01 mm to inside it (the helical
    groove 0.0110 -> 0.0088, a twisted loft 0.0153 -> 0.0099);
  - 29 parts changed; triangles over all 111 parts 1561573 -> 1364975;
  - a module 2, 20-tooth involute gear: 19434 -> 1996 triangles, 76 ->
    21 ms, worst sample 0.0098 -> 0.0048 mm, against 1916 triangles for
    the same gear with its flanks fitted through samples; a smooth loft
    7090 -> 236, a tapered strand 29596 -> 6302;
  - where parts gained triangles they were outside the deflection before
    (helical spring 61720 -> 72754, worst 0.0170 -> 0.0120), and mesh
    time grew with them (626 -> 754 ms); over the corpus it fell 11 %,
    10866 -> 9682 ms, the least of five interleaved runs.
  The eval corpus holds the volumes, areas and sizes it recorded, within
  its 0.05 % and 0.01 mm, except one ellipsoid's mesh volume, which fewer
  triangles at the same deflection moved 0.06 %.
  Change 2 alone moves nothing measurable without change 1: the rows it
  removes were hidden among the nodes the false deflection inserted.

Upstream status
  Not yet offered upstream (github.com/Open-Cascade-SAS/OCCT).
